{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffZ6Ewn9ycze4zbicLQB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a new version of the candidate, and the self-check prints `OK`. It hasn't been through admission yet, so the novelty, convergence and speed gates are still to come.\n\n**Why the first attempt was rejected:** it copied the existing model `tally_span_switch_triplet_variety`, so its predictions matched that model exactly (RMSE 0).\n\n**What changed this time:** the model is still a refinement of the incumbent `iter2_candidate3`, but the change is different. The incumbent rewards switching in a straight line: the more a sequence switches between H and T, the more random it looks. The new model replaces that with closeness to an ideal switching rate. The ideal rate is shared by everyone, with a prior centred around 0.6, and each person has their own weight on it. A sequence that switches too little looks streaky, and one that switches too much, up to strict alternation like HTHTHTHT, looks too regular. Everything else in the incumbent is kept: the span of the running heads-minus-tails tally against each person's expected span, with sensitivity that varies by person and by sequence length, and each person's left/right lean.\n\nThis targets two critique findings that survive the FDR correction:\n- People chose the perfect alternator 0.532 of the time, against the model's predicted 0.598.\n- The alternation preference among pairs that both switch often doesn't follow the incumbent's straight-line reward.\n\n**Self-check:** the smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −2237.4, against −2317.6 for the incumbent. Treat that comparison with caution. It came from a short single-chain fit with 100 draws, R-hat couldn't be computed, and PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable (12.6% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5). It may also be optimistic because I fixed the ideal-rate weight's scale at 4.0 rather than fitting it.\n\nThe model is named